{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/775532-first-change-in-another-pack-s-territory-etiquette","id":775532,"title":"First Change in another pack's territory - Etiquette","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-11-27T10:13:27-08:00","id":775532,"title":"First Change in another pack's territory - Etiquette","content":"We had a new guy join our group recently, and he had his first change under the Full Moon in the pack's territory in his debut game.<br /> <br /> I feel like the player might be a bit upset about how he was treated so I'm curious about what you guys think is common behaviour in this sort of situation. He ended up death raging and our Elodoth pulled him out of it. We then carried him back to her place while he was unconscious and waited for him to wake up.<br /> <br /> They explained what he was, with the requisite song of dance of &quot;that's crazy! Werewolves?&quot; and *TRANSFORM* &quot;Now do you believe?&quot; All that rigamarole.<br /> <br /> Anyway, after that she told him that he was welcome to join the pack and be taught by them, but if he didn't then he would have to leave, as he was in their territory. And she'd give him a couple days to think it over, because of course the decision was up to him.<br /> <br /> She also told him after that that he owed an apology to the member of the pack he almost killed while in death rage, which might have been a weird sticking point for her - but she insisted. He was really upset about that because he didn't remember doing it. (Though he had blood all over him and could taste blood in his mouth). Either way, she wasn't very warm with him (not an asshole, but not exactly welcoming either). Partly because they went through a lot of trouble to subdue him, he is very strong and hurt one of her packmates (and he got blood all over her carpet), we all also had the Exhausted condition as well from a previous battle with Beshilu (poison).<br /> <br /> Anyway, now the player thinks his character wouldn't want to join the pack because of the 'threat' we levied at him and how he was treated. She didn't deliver it as a threat, necessarily, but I mean it's pretty easy to infer that choosing option 3 means being run out of town or worse, I suppose. He's also upset about the apology, as he felt her character should have spent more time explaining to him more about what happened before she insisted on the apology.<br /> <br /> I'm just curious how welcoming werewolves usually are in this situation, whether that threat is usually not broached or stated directly, whether there is usually even a choice, whether the player/character is overreacting (though obviously all these answers might vary from person to person and pack to pack), etc, and how do we sort out this mess we're in?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5279-amravanti","user":"Amravanti","date":". 11-27-2015, 01:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5279-amravanti","name":"Amravanti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T10:35:20-08:00","id":775539,"title":"","content":"Assuming his Blood isn't Lone Wolf, to be so anal about just a request for an apology over almost killing someone is rather... Excessive. He does has a point: He didn't asked to change. Letting a just changed Uratha with very high Harmony wander around who could Kuruth at the moment's notice would be completely irresponsible. So, smack him around, show him how things go, and if it comes to it let him 'take off' but keep an eye on him. Then try to minimize any damage he might cause on his inevitable rampage, and show him the consequences of his recklessness.<br /> <br /> My Elodoth would allow the dude to remain in the turf, wait till he becomes prey to something else, let him have their contact info and all to facilitate watching over. Even wave the whole apology thing if it's such a sore subject. He'll come around eventually.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T10:51:52-08:00","id":775550,"title":"","content":"At first, the character might want to strike out on their own. Then, after failing to deal with their new condition, encountering inhuman enemies or growing homesick, they can turn to the only Werewolves they know. The Pack doesn't need to be a good option, merely the best available one.<br /> <br /> Consider letting the character leave and then making a time-skip until when they return. Ask the new player what plot hook do they want the character to bring back with them. It would be helpful to establish what Packs, Spirits, Humans and Other things are in the area.<br /> <br /> If you want to surprise the older players, ask the new member to make a new character sheet as if to play someone else. Then, after they're convinced the previous character left and feel sorry for mishandling the situation, reintroduce the Werewolf with the plot hook.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T11:16:19-08:00","id":775562,"title":"","content":"It depends.<br /> <br /> A lot would depend on the pack. It's one thing not liking a werewolf. It's another to let someone know they're a werewolf, piss them off, then just send them out into the wild. Lone wolves who don't know what the fuck's going on are easy targets for Pure and manipulative spirits. Suddenly you have a new Fire Touched eager to get out of Omega position by letting his new family know where there's a pack of Forsaken. Or a lone wolf who finds the true way to power is by burning the world down because the Seven Fires of Truth says so.<br /> <br /> Most packs of Forsaken would probably appreciate the danger in such things and be willing to make some exceptions in regards to etiquette for a werewolf who's gone through the First Change and didn't know what they were doing during it.<br /> <br /> In one of our last games we encountered a werewolf, not a 'new' one just new to us. We rescued him from a nasty situation and realized we were sort of working against the same enemy. But he refused to tell us anything about the enemy, or his own allies, implying that this might be a trap and we were really working for the 'bad guy.' We were frustrated, especially as he's our only real edge on the enemy and he clearly doesn't plan on helping us, so we pointed out that the same could be true of him. He was in a nasty situation, sure, but not a lethal one, and now he knows what we're up to and can sneak back and inform Mr. Bad. Things...devolved from there and we went separate ways. One of our supporting wolf-blooded, however, went after him and exchanged some contact information and got them a place to rest and ride, potentially salvaging the situation.<br /> <br /> I think this is an opportunity on your part, providing you're the Storyteller, to take advantage of the support characters, be it totem, wolf-blooded or Other, and have them point out the risks of having a werewolf who knows about them but nothing else walking around on its own. They might also try to convince the pack to be a little more understanding.<br /> <br /> Something else you might do is offer a mystical solution. First edition was full of rites for things like apologies and letting bygones be bygones. Second edition, thankfully, cut that right out, but you could bring some of it back in an example of how ritualized werewolf society is. I wouldn't suggest charging for it, but the most well-studied in the group could figure an ancient rite that reconciles a pack. Or you could tie it into a Sacred Hunt. Let him apologize, or them forgive, by being useful during and trusting one another in the hunt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 11-27-2015, 11:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-02T08:45:40-08:00","id":778347,"title":"","content":"Fancy seeing you here. I clicked on this only because I thought, &quot;Hey this sounds like something that relates to my game I'm playing!&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8177-heroofthemists","name":"Heroofthemists","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"95"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}